N('the-dalles-carnegie-library-or','The Dalles Carnegie Library (The Dalles Art Center)','The Dalles','or','civic','standing',45.59997,-121.18360,'exact','open','walk','','1907–1910',
  "Carnegie's $10,000 grant was approved in March 1907, and the library was dedicated in September 1910. It was the public library until September 1966. The art center leased it from the city in 1967, and in 1997 the city deeded it to The Dalles Art Association, which still runs it. On the National Register since 1978, and in The Dalles Commercial Historic District.",
  'A Beaux-Arts building of brick on concrete foundations, about 50 by 36 feet, with a hip roof and tall brick chimneys with flared tops. Wikipedia says much of the original trim survives, with a fireplace upstairs.',
  '','220 East 4th Street. An art center with galleries; hours from its own page.',
  [W('The Dalles Carnegie Library'),['The Dalles Art Center','http://www.thedallesartcenter.org/'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='Wed–Sat 11–5; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and all of January'),
N('pendleton-carnegie-library-or','Umatilla County Library (Pendleton Center for the Arts)','Pendleton','or','civic','standing',45.67453,-118.78876,'exact','open','walk','','1915–1916',
  "In January 1915 the Carnegie Foundation gave the Umatilla County library board $25,000 for a county library in Pendleton (it had asked for $50,000) and $7,500 for a branch at Milton; the county court added $10,000 to make it fireproof. Folger Johnson of Portland, with Raymond Hatch, drew an Italian Renaissance library for a cramped triangle of ground by the Main Street bridge, and it opened in September 1916. It was the county library until 1987 and then the city's until 1996. The arts council leased and renovated it, and it is now the Pendleton Center for the Arts. On the National Register since 1997.",
  'A Palladian portico on the corner, which the architect said in 1915 was inspired by Brunelleschi\'s Pazzi Chapel in Florence; walls of stucco over hollow clay tile, in smooth and pebbled finishes, and a clay tile roof. Inside are galleries, a 110-seat auditorium and art studios.',
  '','214 North Main Street, at the north end of the Main Street bridge over the Umatilla River. An arts center; hours from its own page.',
  [['Pendleton Center for the Arts','http://www.pendletonarts.org'],W('Pendleton Center for the Arts'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/97000848'],UMA,LK],
  brick='no',hours='Tue–Fri 10–4; Sat 12–4; closed Sun, Mon and major holidays'),
N('union-carnegie-library-or','Union Carnegie Library','Union','or','civic','standing',45.20987,-117.86572,'exact','open','walk','','1911–1912',
  "Carnegie granted $5,500 on April 25, 1911, and the cornerstone shows 1912. The La Grande library's Register form calls Union's library so nearly bare of ornament that it suggests no particular style. The Oregon Encyclopedia and the 2012 passport say it is still a public library, one of the least altered in the state.",
  '',
  '','On North Main Street. A public library; its hours were not checked.',
  [OE,PP,['Oregon Heritage: National Register nomination, La Grande Carnegie Library','https://www.oregon.gov/oprd/OH/Documents/OR_UnionCounty_LaGrandeCarnegieLibrary_form_senttoNPS.pdf'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('woodburn-carnegie-library-or','Woodburn Carnegie Library (Woodburn Public Library)','Woodburn','or','civic','standing',45.14271,-122.85849,'site','open','walk','','1914',
  "Built in 1914 with a $10,000 Carnegie grant (January 14, 1914, says Wikipedia's list). A large addition was built in the late 1970s, and it is still the public library.",
  '',
  '','280 Garfield Street. A public library; its hours were not checked.',
  [PP,OE,LK],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('pacific-university-carnegie-hall','Carnegie Hall (old library), Pacific University','Forest Grove','or','inst','standing',45.5202,-123.11043,'exact','view','walk','','1905–1912',
  "Carnegie granted $20,000 to Pacific University on April 5, 1905, says Wikipedia's list. The brick library, drawn by Whidden and Lewis of Portland, opened in 1912 as the university's first library building. Wikipedia's Hillsboro library article calls it the county's only other Carnegie library, a private one. It is now used for classrooms.",
  '',
  '','2209 College Way, on the Pacific University campus. A college building: look from the campus walks.',
  [W('Pacific University'),PP,LK],brick='?'),
